The session that made the fixes has received your instruction, with your words quoted. It has not reversed anything yet, so nothing is verified.

**What I told it to do**

1. Stop its builds and test runs.
2. Put every file it changed back to how it was, in the app worktree and in the server checkout. In the server checkout it restores only its own files, because other sessions work there.
3. Go through each of the places already identified, on the unchanged code, and write one entry per place: where it is, whose failure it is, the change in one line, and whether that change is simple.
4. Edit nothing, commit nothing, push nothing.

**One thing I added that you did not ask for**

Before discarding, it saves a copy of its work as two patch files in a notes folder outside both repositories. They are for reference only and nobody applies them. Say so if you want them deleted.

**How I defined "simple" for it**

This is my reading, not your wording. A change is simple when it is made at the place itself by adding or removing a try/catch or an if/else, or by deleting handling, using only what the code already has. A place is reported back as needing more when it needs any of these:

- a new helper or shared function;
- new stored state;
- a new field in a message;
- a new sentence or any new translatable text;
- a reporting handler added somewhere other than the place itself;
- a change to what is charged or refunded;
- a change in an OrcaSlicer-owned file.

The two places you marked as not to be fixed are skipped.

**What happens next**

When the session reports, I will check that both checkouts are clean. Then I will bring you the places that need more than a simple change.

