Every change from the fix work is reversed, and I checked it myself. The session then went through the places on the unchanged code without editing anything. Of 50 places, 17 need only a simple change, 30 need more, and 3 need nothing. The list had 42 entries, but three of them held several places each.

**The reversal**
- The app worktree is back at the last commit with no changes.
- The server checkout is back at its last commit. The only things left there belong to other sessions.
- No build is running.
- The discarded work is saved as two patch files in a notes folder outside both repositories.

**The 30 that need more, by what is missing**

- **Nine need the one line of text that shows another side's words**, as in `OpenRouter says: "…"`. You approved that form, and it went away with the reversal. Examples are a full disk during autosave and a refusal from the service behind the person's own key.
- **Nine need a new sentence of ours.** None of them has been seen in the running app:
  - an email address not yet confirmed, or a deactivated account, at sign-in;
  - a Windows computer without WebView2;
  - a chat that is too long;
  - a Klipper printer that needs an API key;
  - the printer window's page errors;
  - a dropped file the page cannot read;
  - an unreadable OrcaSlicer settings file;
  - a declined renewal;
  - an AI provider refusing the person's content.
- **Four repeat every few seconds or on every press**, so reporting them needs a memory of what was already reported: the update check, the AI panel that will not start, the history cleanup after each save, and a kept G-code file that changed.
- **Two tell the page only "worked or not"**, so the page cannot say whose failure it was: opening Credits and plans, and deleting an account.
- **Two need a change in OrcaSlicer's own code**: browser sign-in, and an AI tool exporting to a folder that cannot be written.
- **Four are new behaviour, not a question of who gets the failure**:
  - a project that disappears after an interrupted chat restore;
  - a charge kept when the AI provider fails;
  - the "link has expired" page, which cannot tell a lost link from a late one;
  - a JusPrin 1 payment event that matches no account.

**One thing I found about the four that repeat**

The app's report function already asks about the same failure only once per run when it shows the dialog (`ErrorReport.cpp:112`). With automatic reports on, it sends every repeat. I read this in the code and did not run it. One check in that one place might cover all the repeating places. That is a proposal, not something built.

**The 3 that need nothing**
- A print report with no answer, by your earlier word.
- A failed tool call, which the rules already cover.
- A report that itself cannot be sent.

**What matters most**

34 of the 50 places were never made to happen against the real app or server. Of the 17 simple ones, I count 13. The list came from people reading code, not from failures anyone saw. Your rule says no fix for a bug that was not reproduced. So even a simple change waits until its failure has been produced once, and a place that cannot be made to happen stays as it is and is listed.

Should I make the 17 simple changes, each only after its failure has been made to happen: yes, the 17, or not yet?

